# Open Watcom 2.0 makefile for the DOS target.
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos            both of the below (the default)
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos game       the game
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos modes      MODES.EXE, the VBE mode lister
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos check      just the DOS-only files, compiled
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos clean
#
# Both targets are verified: the game runs on DOS 7.10 hardware, on FreeDOS
# under QEMU and under DOSBox-X. This file began as a proposal written by
# reading the Win32 Makefile beside it, and the flags below have since been
# run.
#
# The extender it links against is DOS/32A. `Makefile.cw` is this same file
# with five values overridden for CauseWay -- not a copy of it; see the note
# above LSYSTEM for which macros are parameterised and why the objects are
# deliberately not among them.
#
# MODES.EXE links src/vbe.c and src/vbedos.c and nothing else, which makes
# it both a useful tool on the target -- it prints every mode the BIOS
# offers and, for each one refused, the reason -- and the smallest possible
# check that the toolchain, the extender, the DPMI calls and the real-mode
# scratch buffer all work.
#
# Build on Linux with the Linux-hosted Open Watcom tools, as the Win32 build
# is built:
#
#     export WATCOM=/home/fosco/bin/WATCOM
#     export PATH=$WATCOM/binl:$PATH
#     export INCLUDE=$WATCOM/h
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos
#
# Note INCLUDE has no $WATCOM/h/nt on it, unlike the Win32 build: that
# directory is the Win32 API headers and nothing here wants them.

# --- the extender ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# DOS/32A, bound into the executable, so what ships is one file that needs
# nothing on the path.
#
# `system dos32a` does all of it. An earlier version of this file claimed
# there was no such target and hand-rolled it as
#
#     wlink system dos4g option stub=dos32a.exe
#
# with an invented path to dos32a.exe, which was wrong twice over. wlink.lnk
# in $WATCOM/binl has had the target all along:
#
#     system begin dos32a
#         option osname='DOS/32 Advanced DOS Extender (LE-style)'
#         libpath '%WATCOM%/lib386'
#         libpath '%WATCOM%/lib386/dos'
#         libpath '%WATCOM%/lib386/l32'
#         op stub=dos32a.exe
#         format os2 le
#     end
#
# Note that its `op stub=dos32a.exe` carries no path, and on *this* host
# that does not resolve. The Linux-hosted wlink lives in $WATCOM/binl,
# while dos32a.exe is in $WATCOM/binw with the DOS- and Windows-hosted
# binaries; on a DOS or Windows host the two share a directory and the bare
# name finds it. Cross-building from Linux, it does not:
#
#     creating a DOS/32 Advanced DOS Extender (LE-style) executable
#     Warning! W1093: cannot open dos32a.exe
#
# So the path is supplied on the command line, where it overrides the
# system definition's default. Open Watcom does ship DOS/32A -- dos32a.exe
# and sb.exe sit beside dos4gw.exe -- so there is still nothing to install.
#
# And the reason the recipe tests for the file first: **wlink does not fail
# on a missing stub.** It prints that warning, links the default DOS/4GW
# stub instead, and exits successfully, so a bad path yields a binary that
# needs DOS4GW.EXE at run time from a build that looks like it worked.
# Two different wrong paths got past this before the test was added.
STOCK_STUB = $(%WATCOM)/binw/dos32a.exe

# The bound extender is a copy of Open Watcom's with six bytes changed, so
# that it looks for a DPMI host *before* a VCPI server. Stock DOS/32A takes
# VCPI whenever it is offered, and a VCPI client is invisible to VSBHDA's
# port trapping -- which is how you get "no Sound Blaster at 220" from a
# machine that is displaying "SB emulation: Addr=220". tools/mkdpmifirst.py
# has the whole story, including why the extender's own documented option
# for this (/DPMITST, and the field in ss.exe) does nothing in 9.1.2.
#
# It is the default because it was measured to change nothing where there is
# no DPMI host to find: full runs on the PIII under DOS 7.10 with
# himem+emm386 reporting the full memory, on DOSBox-X, and on FreeDOS in
# QEMU with a stock Jemm configuration at both 64MB and 256MB -- that last
# one with working audio and no JEMMEX NOVCPI.
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos game STUB=$(STOCK_STUB)
#
# backs it out, and is the first thing to try if an extender-level problem
# ever shows up on a machine this has not been run on.
DPMI_STUB  = dos32adp.exe
!ifndef STUB
STUB       = $(DPMI_STUB)
!endif
#
# `system dos32x` is the same thing in LX format and `system stub32a` uses
# DOS/32A's smaller standard stub, which loads DOS32A.EXE from disk rather
# than carrying it. Neither is wanted here: LE is the format DOS/4GW
# compatibility is defined in, and the whole point is to carry the
# extender.
#
# SB.EXE ("stub it") does the same binding after the link and is not used:
# it is a DOS program, and this build runs on Linux.
#
# LSYSTEM, STUB, GAME_TARGET, GAME_MAP and MODES are each set only if they
# are not set already, so a second extender is a short wrapper over this file
# rather than a copy of it -- see Makefile.cw. The objects are deliberately
# *not* parameterised: the compile is identical for any DOS extender (same
# -bt=dos, same GCFLAGS), so both builds share the one set of .do files and
# only the link differs. That is the opposite of the .o/.do split against the
# Win32 build, where the compile genuinely differs and sharing would be a
# wrong binary.
!ifndef LSYSTEM
LSYSTEM = dos32a
!endif

CC   = wcc386
LINK = wlink

# -bt=dos  target 32-bit DOS rather than NT
# -6r      Pentium Pro scheduling, register calling convention
# -fp5     Pentium FPU code; the target is a Pentium III
# -otexan  full speed optimisation
# -sg      grow the stack -- see the long note in the Win32 Makefile, which
#          applies here too and possibly more: a DPMI host commits the stack
#          lazily just as Windows 9x does, so a function whose frame exceeds
#          a page without touching it in order faults. There are 21 such
#          frames in the engine as of this writing; tools/stackprobe.py
#          counts them and checks every one has its __GRO probe.
# -zq      quiet
# -w4 -e25 warnings on, stop after 25 errors
# -d1      line-number debug info, harmless in the shipped binary
#
# Deliberately *not* -fpi87 here, unlike the Win32 build. That one drops the
# emulator reference because Win32s guarantees an FPU; under bare DOS the
# runtime's emulator fallback costs nothing if an FPU is present and is the
# difference between running and not on a machine without one. The target is
# a Pentium III, so this is insurance rather than a requirement -- but it is
# free insurance, which the Win32 note explicitly says the other one is not.
CFLAGS = -bt=dos -6r -fp5 -otexan -sg -zq -w4 -e25 -d1 -i=src

MODES_OBJS = tools/modes$(DOSOBJ) src/vbe$(DOSOBJ) &
             src/vbedos$(DOSOBJ) src/dosmem$(DOSOBJ)

# Guarded like GAME_TARGET: a second extender's MODES.EXE is a different
# binary and must not overwrite this one, since comparing the two is the
# first thing you want when an extender misbehaves.
!ifndef MODES
MODES = modes.exe
!endif

# probe/memwalk.c: the DPMI host honesty check. It is a DOS program with the
# same three engine modules MODES.EXE needs, so it belongs here rather than
# in probe/Makefile, which is GNU make for the *host* probes and says so.
#
# Built by this file rather than by the hand-written wcc386/wlink pair that
# used to sit in its header, because that pair named `system dos32a` and a
# literal stub path: run it after switching extenders and you would measure
# DOS/32A's memory manager while believing you were measuring CauseWay's,
# which is precisely the question the probe exists to answer.
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos memwalk     memwalk.exe,   DOS/32A
#     wmake -f Makefile.cw  memwalk     memwalkcw.exe, CauseWay
#
# Deliberately not in `all`: it is a diagnostic, not something the DOS build
# needs, and it is wanted about once per memory manager.
MEMWALK_OBJS = probe/memwalk$(DOSOBJ) src/vbe$(DOSOBJ) &
               src/vbedos$(DOSOBJ) src/dosmem$(DOSOBJ)

!ifndef MEMWALK
MEMWALK = memwalk.exe
!endif

# --- mmxcheck --------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos mmxcheck    mmxchk.exe,   DOS/32A
#     wmake -f Makefile.cw  mmxcheck    mmxchkcw.exe, CauseWay
#
# Does the hand-written MMX colour conversion agree with the C one?
#
# This is the only thing anywhere that can answer that. src/yuvmmx.c is
# Watcom inline assembly, so the gcc harness suite cannot compile it, let
# alone run it -- yuvtest says as much at the end of its output rather than
# letting its green lines imply coverage it has not got. The *arithmetic* is
# proved on the host, exhaustively (see src/yuvmmx.h); the transcription into
# assembly can only be checked on a machine with MMX in it.
#
# Deliberately not in `all`, like memwalk: it is wanted once after a change to
# the kernel, not on every build. But unlike memwalk it is wanted at least
# once, because until it has been run the kernel is written-but-unproven.
#
# It links the conversion and the surfaces and nothing else -- no presenter,
# no decoder, no VBE. The subject is one loop.
# src/cpu$(DOSOBJ) because yuv.c's mr_yuv_have_simd() now asks the processor
# as well as the build. That is also why the probe is worth more than it was:
# it links the same predicate the engine uses, so if detection says no on this
# machine the probe says "skipped" instead of "ok", which is a result and not
# a silence.
MMXCHECK_OBJS = probe/mmxcheck$(DOSOBJ) src/yuv$(DOSOBJ) &
                src/yuvmmx$(DOSOBJ) src/gfx$(DOSOBJ) src/cpu$(DOSOBJ)

!ifndef MMXCHECK
MMXCHECK = mmxchk.exe
!endif

# --- blendcheck ------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos blendcheck  blendchk.exe,  DOS/32A
#     wmake -f Makefile.cw  blendcheck  blndchkcw.exe, CauseWay
#
# Does the hand-written MMX alpha blend agree with the C one? Same
# arrangement as mmxcheck above and for the same reason -- src/blendmmx.c is
# Watcom inline assembly, so the gcc harness suite cannot compile it, let
# alone run it. blendtest says as much at the end of its output rather than
# letting its green lines imply coverage it has not got. The lane arithmetic
# is proved on the host, exhaustively over all 16,777,216 (src, dst, alpha)
# triples (see src/blendmmx.h); the transcription into assembly can only be
# checked on a machine with MMX in it.
#
# Deliberately not in `all`: wanted once after a change to the kernel. But
# wanted at least once, because until it has been run the kernel is
# written-but-unproven.
#
# It links the surfaces and the kernel and nothing else -- no presenter, no
# UI, no interpreter. The subject is one loop.
BLENDCHECK_OBJS = probe/blendchk$(DOSOBJ) src/gfx$(DOSOBJ) &
                  src/blendmmx$(DOSOBJ) src/cpu$(DOSOBJ)

!ifndef BLENDCHECK
BLENDCHECK = blendchk.exe
!endif

# --- cpucheck --------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos cpucheck    cpuchk.exe,   DOS/32A
#     wmake -f Makefile.cw  cpucheck    cpuchkcw.exe, CauseWay
#
# One layer under the two above: the gate that decides whether either kernel
# runs at all. src/cpu.c asks CPUID, following xvidcore's cpuid.asm, and both
# mr_yuv_have_simd() and mr_blend_have_simd() now go through it -- so a
# detector that answers wrongly turns off two proven kernels, or turns them on
# where the opcodes do not exist.
#
# Its decode is proved on the host by cputest, over synthesised leaf values.
# This is the half that needs a processor: that the values reaching the decode
# came off CPUID correctly, and that MMX detected is MMX that works. See the
# header of probe/cpuchk.c on why only one direction of that is checkable.
#
# **Two object files.** That is deliberate and is the reason cpu.c is its own
# module rather than a function in gfx.c: the thing gating the fast paths
# should be checkable without linking the compositor.
CPUCHECK_OBJS = probe/cpuchk$(DOSOBJ) src/cpu$(DOSOBJ)

!ifndef CPUCHECK
CPUCHECK = cpuchk.exe
!endif

# memwalk.c alone is built unoptimised; MW_CFLAGS and its rule are down with
# the mp3impl rule, because a target naming $(DOSOBJ) has to come after
# DOSOBJ is defined.

# The default target, and so the first rule in the file: both executables.
# MODES.EXE is small, shares every object it uses with the game, and is the
# first thing wanted on a machine where the game picked the wrong mode --
# so there is no reason for `wmake -f Makefile.dos` on its own to build
# only one of them.
all: .SYMBOLIC game modes

modes: .SYMBOLIC $(MODES)

memwalk: .SYMBOLIC $(MEMWALK)

mmxcheck: .SYMBOLIC $(MMXCHECK)

blendcheck: .SYMBOLIC $(BLENDCHECK)

cpucheck: .SYMBOLIC $(CPUCHECK)

# --- the game --------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos game
#
# Objects end in .do, not .o, and that is the important detail in this whole
# file. The Win32 build compiles the same sources to src/audio.o; if this one
# did too, the two builds would overwrite each other's objects and -- far
# worse -- each would then find the other's up to date and link it. A DOS
# object in a Win32 exe does not fail to link in any obvious way. Separate
# extensions mean both builds can live in the tree at once, which is what
# actually happens here.
#
# The same reasoning covers zlib and libpng: their sources are compiled
# directly into .do objects beside the .o ones the Win32 build makes, rather
# than through their own makefiles, which have one fixed output name each and
# would have the same collision one level down.
DOSOBJ = .do

ZLIB = libs/zlib-1.4
PNG  = libs/libpng-1.6.59
XVID = libs/xvidcore-1.3.7

# xvidcore for DOS is a *library*, not a list of objects compiled in place
# like zlib's and libpng's above. Its sources are spread over eight
# subdirectories, which the `.c$(DOSOBJ)` extension rule and its path
# specifier cannot reach, and Makefile.watcom in the xvid tree already has an
# explicit rule for every one of them -- so this build calls that rather than
# restating fifty rules in a second dialect.
#
# **Pure C, no MMX**, unlike the Win32 build's copy of the same library. The
# assembly is nasm emitting win32 COFF with OpenWatcom cdecl naming and has
# never been checked against a flat DOS target; the reasoning is written out
# at the top of Makefile.watcom. The DOS port keeps most of the benefit --
# xvidcore's C decoder is about twice pl_mpeg's speed on the same content,
# see src/viddec.h -- and loses the MMX multiplier on top of it.
#
# In build/dos, beside the Win32 build's rather than inside it: the two used
# to share build/win32 and therefore shared object names, and the DOS target
# cleaned first so it would not archive -bt=nt objects. That clean destroyed
# the Win32 library, and the Win32 engine then failed to link with undefined
# references that looked like a code fault. Separate directories now, so
# neither build can touch the other.
# Which xvidcore to build and link. One macro, because the directory, the
# library name and the wmake target all have to agree and having them written
# out separately meant they could disagree -- which they promptly did: the
# recipe below built `dossimd` while the path still said `dos`, so the library
# was built correctly and then the guard looked for one that was never made.
#
#   dossimd   the same 58 SIMD objects the Win32 library has. The default,
#             measured on the PIII at 8.47ms a frame against the pure C
#             build's 12.32ms, with xvidcore reporting `simd=sse`.
#   dos       pure C, no assembly. The fallback, and still useful: it is the
#             one thing that separates "xvidcore's SIMD is wrong on this
#             machine" from "the decoder is wrong", without a bisect.
#
# Switch with one word and nothing can fall out of step:
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos XVIDVARIANT=dos
#
# The default was `dos` until the SIMD build was shown to run. Worth noting
# what made that safe rather than hopeful: xvidcore picks its kernels at run
# time and falls back to C for whatever the machine lacks, and for SSE it does
# not trust CPUID alone -- it executes a trial instruction under a SIGILL
# handler first (xvid.c:152), because SSE also needs the OS to have set
# CR4.OSFXSR. On this target that trial *passes*, so the answer is `sse` and
# not the `mmxext` that was predicted here; DOS/32A evidently does set it.
!ifndef XVIDVARIANT
XVIDVARIANT = dossimd
!endif

XVIDLIB = $(XVID)/build/$(XVIDVARIANT)/xvidcore_$(XVIDVARIANT).lib

# wmake has to be told the extension exists before a rule can name it. The
# first line clears the default list, which is what makes the order below the
# search order; .c has to be in it or nothing builds at all.
.EXTENSIONS:
.EXTENSIONS: .do .c

# And it has to be told where the sources are, even though every target below
# carries its own directory: without a path specifier naming the directory, a
# target in it is "does not exist and cannot be made from existing files".
# Tried and watched fail, which is the only way to find that out.
#
# Spelled literally rather than through $(DOSOBJ), because an extension rule
# is parsed before macros of that kind are any use -- `.c$(DOSOBJ):` is not
# recognised as a rule at all, and the build fails exactly as it does with no
# rule present.
.c: src;tools;probe;$(ZLIB);$(PNG)

.c.do:
	$(CC) $(GCFLAGS) -fo=$^@ $^*.c

# -mf      flat memory model, as the Win32 build uses; DOS/32A is flat too
# -i=      libpng includes zlib's headers, and src/png.c includes libpng's
#
# MR_TARGET_DOS is what switches vbedos.c, presdos.c, platdos.c, wavedos.c,
# dosmem.c and vfs.c's loose-file enumeration to their DOS halves. Watcom's
# own __DOS__ would do under -bt=dos; naming it ourselves means the same
# sources can be built for DOS by another compiler without silently
# compiling to stubs, which is the failure that looks like "the BIOS reports
# no modes" rather than like a build problem.
# -DMR_YUV_MMX: the hand-written MMX colour conversion in src/yuvmmx.c,
# which is video playback's inner loop. The PIII baseline has MMX, so it is
# on here and in the Win32 Makefile, and never defined for the gcc harness
# build -- that one cannot compile Watcom inline assembly, and a kernel the
# suite appears to cover but does not is worse than one it plainly does not.
# mrpy.ini's `simd=0` turns it off at run time; probe/mmxcheck.c is what
# checks it against the C path, on the machine.
# -DMR_BLEND_MMX: the same arrangement for the MMX alpha blend in
# src/blendmmx.c, the compositor's last compute-bound loop -- the textbox
# panel's 148,000 partial-alpha pixels at 126.8 ns/px, 18.8ms on every frame
# that is not a cached dialogue band. Same `simd` key, but read at startup in
# main.c, because unlike the colour conversion this one runs from frame one.
# src/blendmmx.h has the lane arithmetic; probe/blendchk.c is the half only
# the machine can answer.
GCFLAGS = $(CFLAGS) -mf -DMR_TARGET_DOS -DMR_YUV_MMX -DMR_BLEND_MMX -i=$(PNG) -i=$(ZLIB) &
          -i=libs -i=$(XVID)/src

!ifndef GAME_TARGET
GAME_TARGET = mrpydos.exe
!endif

# Kept beside GAME_TARGET rather than written into the link rule, because a
# second build out of the same objects is a one-line override and the map is
# how a crash address becomes a function name. Overwriting mrpydos.map from
# a differently-linked binary would silently answer the wrong question:
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos game LSYSTEM=dos4g &
#           STUB=$(%WATCOM)/binw/wstub.exe &
#           GAME_TARGET=miwa4g.exe GAME_MAP=miwa4g.map
#
# That one produces a DOS/4GW binary, which needs DOS4GW.EXE beside it or on
# the PATH -- wstub.exe loads the extender from disk rather than carrying it,
# unlike the bound dos32a.exe stub this file uses by default.
#
# Makefile.cw is that same override written down and kept, for CauseWay.
!ifndef GAME_MAP
GAME_MAP = mrpydos.map
!endif

# The same list the Win32 Makefile keeps, minus presw32.h and plus the DOS
# ones. Two copies, which is a wart: they are the input to the invalidation
# stamp below and nothing else, so the cost of them drifting apart is a
# rebuild that is too coarse or not coarse enough, not a wrong binary.
HDRS = src/audio.h src/config.h src/expr.h src/font.h src/history.h &
       src/image.h src/interp.h src/lex.h src/list.h src/md5.h src/mr.h &
       src/musicroom.h src/persist.h src/present.h src/pyfn.h src/rhythm.h &
       src/save.h src/scale.h src/scene.h src/script.h src/serial.h &
       src/slang.h &
       src/slparse.h src/slrender.h src/stmtid.h src/style.h src/tl.h &
       src/ui.h src/bandcache.h src/blendmmx.h src/cpu.h src/vfs.h &
       src/datefmt.h &
       src/plat.h src/input.h src/keys.h src/pack.h src/wave.h src/sbconv.h &
       src/presdos.h src/cursor.h src/dosmem.h src/dosisr.h src/vbe.h &
       src/viddec.h src/xviddec.h src/avi.h

GAME_OBJS = src/main$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/platdos$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/presdos$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/wavedos$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/sbconv$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/dosmem$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/vbe$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/vbedos$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/cursordat$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/input$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/gfx$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/scale$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/pack$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/png$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/image$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/lex$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/parse$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/expr$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/scene$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/interp$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/audio$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/font$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/ui$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/bandcache$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/vfs$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/mp3impl$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/plmimpl$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/avi$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/xviddec$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/viddec$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/yuv$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/yuvmmx$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/blendmmx$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/cpu$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/movie$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/slang$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/slparse$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/style$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/slrender$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/save$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/history$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/serial$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/datefmt$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/stmtid$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/tl$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/md5$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/persist$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/musicroom$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/pyfn$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/rhythm$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/list$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/config$(DOSOBJ) &
            src/ansi$(DOSOBJ)

ZLIB_OBJS = $(ZLIB)/adler32$(DOSOBJ)  $(ZLIB)/compress$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/crc32$(DOSOBJ)    $(ZLIB)/deflate$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/gzclose$(DOSOBJ)  $(ZLIB)/gzlib$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/gzread$(DOSOBJ)   $(ZLIB)/gzwrite$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/infback$(DOSOBJ)  $(ZLIB)/inffast$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/inflate$(DOSOBJ)  $(ZLIB)/inftrees$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/trees$(DOSOBJ)    $(ZLIB)/uncompr$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(ZLIB)/zutil$(DOSOBJ)

PNG_OBJS  = $(PNG)/png$(DOSOBJ)      $(PNG)/pngerror$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngget$(DOSOBJ)   $(PNG)/pngmem$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngpread$(DOSOBJ) $(PNG)/pngread$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngrio$(DOSOBJ)   $(PNG)/pngrtran$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngrutil$(DOSOBJ) $(PNG)/pngset$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngtrans$(DOSOBJ) $(PNG)/pngwio$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngwrite$(DOSOBJ) $(PNG)/pngwtran$(DOSOBJ) &
            $(PNG)/pngwutil$(DOSOBJ)

game: .SYMBOLIC $(GAME_TARGET)

# libpng's pnglibconf.h has to say PNG_API_RULE 2 -- Watcom calling
# conventions -- or a -6r engine will not link against it. The top-level
# Makefile derives it from the shipped prebuilt with that one value patched;
# the same rule is here so this target does not depend on the Win32 build
# having been run first. Both write the same file from the same source, so
# whichever runs first wins and the other does nothing.
$(PNG)/pnglibconf.h: $(PNG)/scripts/pnglibconf.h.prebuilt
	sed -e "s/PNG_API_RULE 0/PNG_API_RULE 2/" $(PNG)/scripts/pnglibconf.h.prebuilt > $^@
	grep -q "PNG_API_RULE 2" $^@

# minimp3 is C99 -- declarations after statements -- so its one translation
# unit needs -za99. Everything else stays C89. -w1 because it is somebody
# else's code and -w4 on it is noise.
src/mp3impl$(DOSOBJ): src/mp3impl.c
	$(CC) $(GCFLAGS) -za99 -w1 -fo=$^@ $^*.c

# pl_mpeg, the MPEG-1 decoder, is C99 too and more so -- `//` comments
# throughout, declarations inside `for`. Same treatment, same reason, and this
# rule is why src/plmdec.h exists: movie.c is where every decision about
# timing and frame dropping is made, so it has to be readable under the
# engine's C89 rule, and it cannot be if it includes the vendor header. This
# is the only translation unit that does.
#
# Like the memwalk rule below, this must sit *after* `DOSOBJ = .do`. A target
# naming $(DOSOBJ) above that line expands to `src/plmimpl` with no extension,
# the .c.do extension rule wins instead, and the object is quietly built
# without -za99 -- which on this file is several hundred syntax errors, so it
# would at least be loud. The same mistake on a file that merely wanted
# different optimisation was silent; see memwalk.
src/plmimpl$(DOSOBJ): src/plmimpl.c
	$(CC) $(GCFLAGS) -za99 -w1 -fo=$^@ $^*.c

# probe/memwalk.c alone is built *unoptimised*, which is not an oversight.
#
# It stamps pages, calls out to map the framebuffer, and reads them back, and
# declares nothing volatile. Today -otexan cannot legally cache those reads
# across an opaque call, so it would probably survive it -- "probably" being
# the problem. probe/Makefile makes the same choice for sccheck and stmtdump
# and gives the reason: a probe exists to catch a write that nothing should
# have made, and an optimiser holding the watched value in a register is the
# one thing that would make it lie. A probe you must reason about before
# trusting is worth less than one built the dull way.
#
# It also keeps the result comparable with the measurement in NOTES
# ("realloc to 1088KB lost 556819 of 557056 bytes"), which came from the
# hand-written wcc386 line that used to live in memwalk.c's header -- and
# that line carried no -otexan, no -fp5 and no -sg either. The three engine
# modules linked beside it stay on GCFLAGS: they are shared with the game and
# MODES.EXE, and the sensitive loops are not in them.
#
# This rule lives here, after DOSOBJ is defined, and that is load-bearing.
# Put it up beside MEMWALK_OBJS and the target expands to `probe/memwalk`
# with no extension, the .c.do extension rule wins instead, and the object is
# quietly built with -otexan -- which this file already warns about for
# `.c$(DOSOBJ):`. Caught by reading the dry run, not by reasoning: the
# compile line said -otexan while the makefile said -od.
MW_CFLAGS = -bt=dos -6r -od -zq -w4 -e25 -d1 -i=src -mf -DMR_TARGET_DOS

probe/memwalk$(DOSOBJ): probe/memwalk.c
	$(CC) $(MW_CFLAGS) -fo=$^@ $^*.c

# The engine objects depend on every header, the same sledgehammer the Win32
# Makefile uses and for the same reason written up there: mr_value carries
# its string inline, so a constant in a header changes struct layouts, and a
# build that relinks stale objects against fresh ones produces a binary that
# runs until the first module boundary. Deleting them all is coarse and the
# tree compiles in seconds.
#
# A separate stamp from the Win32 build's: the two have different object
# lists, and sharing one would have each build delete the other's work.
hdrstamp.dos: $(HDRS)
	rm -f $(GAME_OBJS)
	touch hdrstamp.dos

# No `system dos32a` library list to prune, unlike the Win32 build's
# mrpy.lnk: a DOS program imports nothing.
#
# `option stack` is the one thing here that is not obvious. DOS/32A's default
# is 64KB and the engine needs more than that -- an expression frame carries
# mr_value argv[8], which is 8.4KB, so nested evaluation descends in large
# steps. 1MB to match what the Win32 link reserves, and under DOS/32A it is
# committed rather than grown through a guard page, which is one less thing
# that can go differently between the two builds.
# $(STUB) is a prerequisite so that `game STUB=dos32adp.exe` generates the
# patched extender rather than failing the check below.
# The grep guards against an archive that exists and is empty, which satisfies
# this target and then fails the engine link on an undefined xvid_global --
# an error that reads as a code fault and is not one. Same idiom and same
# reason as the Win32 Makefile's.
$(XVIDLIB):
	cd $(XVID)/build/win32 && wmake -h -f Makefile.watcom $(XVIDVARIANT)
	grep -aq xvid_global $(XVIDLIB)

$(GAME_TARGET): hdrstamp.dos $(PNG)/pnglibconf.h $(STUB) $(GAME_OBJS) &
                $(ZLIB_OBJS) $(PNG_OBJS) $(XVIDLIB)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB)" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(GAME_TARGET) &
		option stack=1m, map=$(GAME_MAP), eliminate, quiet &
		file {$(GAME_OBJS)} &
		file {$(ZLIB_OBJS)} &
		file {$(PNG_OBJS)} &
		library $(XVIDLIB)
	@echo
	@echo "Built $(GAME_TARGET). It wants mrpy.pak beside it, or a game"
	@echo "directory as its one argument. The pak is the same file the"
	@echo "Win32 build uses; 'wmake data' over there is what makes it."

# MODES.EXE's objects are built by the same `.c.do:` rule as everything else
# -- `tools` is on the path specifier above for modes.c's sake.
#
# It was three explicit rules once, written that way because this makefile
# could not be tested at the time and explicit rules avoid the parts of wmake
# that have to be exactly right. They can go now that the game target has
# made the extension rule work for seventy other files. The history is worth
# one line: the first attempt here failed with "(tools/modes.o) does not
# exist and cannot be made from existing files" because the path specifier
# did not name tools/, which is the error wmake gives for a target whose
# directory it was not told about.

# The stub is checked before the link and not after, because wlink turns a
# missing one into a warning plus a DOS/4GW binary rather than an error.
#
# One line, no backslash continuations: wmake's line-continuation character
# is `&`, and a `\` ending a recipe line is not reliably handed to the
# shell as one.
$(MODES): $(STUB) $(MODES_OBJS)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB) -- Open Watcom ships them in binw beside dos4gw.exe (dos32a.exe, cwstub.exe), so check that WATCOM is set; the DOS/32A default is generated from $(STOCK_STUB) by tools/mkdpmifirst.py; or pass STUB=/path/to/stub.exe" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(MODES) file {$(MODES_OBJS)}
	@echo
	@echo "Built $(MODES) for '$(LSYSTEM)' with $(STUB) bound in."
	@echo "One file; nothing needed on the path at run time."

# Same shape as $(MODES), same stub check, same reason for it. `option
# stack=1m` is not here: memwalk's own frames are small and the thing it
# measures is the heap.
# No `option map=`: the obvious spelling gives memwalk.exe.map, a sixth
# override would be needed to avoid it, and a probe that prints its own
# diagnosis has never wanted a map.
$(MMXCHECK): $(STUB) $(MMXCHECK_OBJS)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB)" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(MMXCHECK) &
		option eliminate, quiet &
		file {$(MMXCHECK_OBJS)}

$(CPUCHECK): $(STUB) $(CPUCHECK_OBJS)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB)" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(CPUCHECK) &
		option eliminate, quiet &
		file {$(CPUCHECK_OBJS)}

$(BLENDCHECK): $(STUB) $(BLENDCHECK_OBJS)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB)" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(BLENDCHECK) &
		option eliminate, quiet &
		file {$(BLENDCHECK_OBJS)}

$(MEMWALK): $(STUB) $(MEMWALK_OBJS)
	@test -f "$(STUB)" || ( echo "no '$(LSYSTEM)' stub at $(STUB)" ; exit 1 )
	$(LINK) system $(LSYSTEM) option stub=$(STUB) name $(MEMWALK) &
		option eliminate, quiet &
		file {$(MEMWALK_OBJS)}
	@echo
	@echo "Built $(MEMWALK) for '$(LSYSTEM)' with $(STUB) bound in."
	@echo "Usage: $(MEMWALK) [megabytes] [file-to-read]   (default 96, the pak)"

# --- just the DOS-only files -----------------------------------------------
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos check
#
# The six files that exist only for this target, compiled on their own.
# `game` builds them too, along with seventy others, and links them; this is
# for when the question is "does the DOS platform code still compile" and the
# answer is wanted in two seconds rather than in a minute.
#
# They are the files where it matters most. The host's gcc can be pointed at
# them -- run.sh does -- but only through a shim that defines __interrupt,
# __loadds and __far away, so what it checks is spelling. Everything here
# that is actually hard about them is invisible to it.
CHECK_OBJS = src/presdos$(DOSOBJ) src/platdos$(DOSOBJ) &
             src/wavedos$(DOSOBJ) src/sbconv$(DOSOBJ) &
             src/dosmem$(DOSOBJ) src/cursordat$(DOSOBJ)

check: .SYMBOLIC $(CHECK_OBJS)
	@echo
	@echo "Compiled the DOS platform files. Use 'game' to link them."


# --- the mouse pointer -----------------------------------------------------
#
# DOS has no cursor to borrow, so the engine draws one, and the art for it is
# a PNG baked into a C array at build time rather than pixels typed into a
# source file. Point CURSOR_PNG at another file and rebuild -- a game with a
# reason to have its own pointer changes this one line.
#
# HOTSPOT is where the pointer actually points, in pixels from the top left of
# the image. 0,0 suits an arrow whose tip is its first pixel; a round one
# wants its middle, and getting it wrong means clicks land a few pixels from
# where they look, which is maddening and nearly invisible.
#
# The default art comes from tools/mkarrow.py, which writes tools/cursor.png
# from an ASCII drawing. The PNG is in the repository, so a build does not
# need Python unless the art changes; `wmake -f Makefile.dos arrow` regenerates
# it.
CURSOR_PNG = tools/cursor.png
HOTSPOT    = 0 0
PYTHON     = python3

src/cursordat.c: $(CURSOR_PNG) tools/mkcursor.py
	$(PYTHON) tools/mkcursor.py $(CURSOR_PNG) $^@ $(HOTSPOT)


arrow: .SYMBOLIC
	$(PYTHON) tools/mkarrow.py tools/cursor.png

# The DPMI-first extender. Six bytes of Open Watcom's own dos32a.exe, so it
# is generated rather than checked in, and the tool refuses to write
# anything it is not certain about.
$(DPMI_STUB): tools/mkdpmifirst.py $(STOCK_STUB)
	$(PYTHON) tools/mkdpmifirst.py $(STOCK_STUB) $^@

dpmistub: .SYMBOLIC $(DPMI_STUB)
	@echo "Built $(DPMI_STUB). Use it with:"
	@echo "    wmake -f Makefile.dos game STUB=$(DPMI_STUB)"

# Everything this makefile can make, so that
#
#     wmake -f Makefile.dos clean ; wmake -f Makefile.dos game
#
# really does rebuild the game from source rather than relinking whatever
# objects happened to survive. That includes the generated cursor table, the
# header stamp -- without which the next build thinks the headers have not
# changed since objects that no longer exist -- and the libraries' .do
# objects, which are ours: the Win32 build's .o files next to them are not
# touched, which is the whole point of the two extensions.
clean: .SYMBOLIC
	rm -f $(MODES_OBJS) $(CHECK_OBJS) $(MODES)
	rm -f $(MEMWALK_OBJS) $(MEMWALK)
	rm -f probe/mmxcheck$(DOSOBJ) $(MMXCHECK)
	rm -f probe/blendchk$(DOSOBJ) $(BLENDCHECK)
	rm -f probe/cpuchk$(DOSOBJ) $(CPUCHECK)
	rm -f $(GAME_OBJS) $(ZLIB_OBJS) $(PNG_OBJS)
	rm -f $(GAME_TARGET) $(GAME_MAP) src/cursordat.c hdrstamp.dos
	rm -f $(DPMI_STUB)
	rm -f *.err

# --- the hang at exit ------------------------------------------------------
#
# A long note used to sit here describing MODES.EXE hanging after its last
# printf as an open problem with a hypothesis. It is solved and the writeup
# has moved to NOTES.md, "The hang at exit, and segment-versus-selector
# again": vbedos.c was freeing DPMI 0100h's real-mode *segment* through
# 0101h, which frees a *selector*, so it released a descriptor belonging to
# something else and left the MCB chain to loop forever. Both calls are DPMI
# by hand now, keeping both numbers in a named struct.
#
# The one line worth repeating here, because it is about to happen again: a
# segment and a selector are both `unsigned short`, and interchanging them
# compiles in silence. It has caused a GP fault one way round and this hang
# the other, in the same file.

dist: .SYMBOLIC $(GAME_TARGET)
        mkdir -p dist
        cp $(GAME_TARGET) dist/
        upx --strip-relocs=0 dist/$(GAME_TARGET)
